# Example env file for ops-alerts.service. Copy to /etc/transit/ops-alerts.env
# (mode 0600) only if you need to override a default -- unlike ops-status.env,
# nothing here is a secret.

# Only needed if this service's deployed checkout differs from
# ops-alerts.service's own WorkingDirectory= default (/root/transit-app).
# OPS_ALERT_REPO=/root/transit-app

# owner/repo slug for the GitHub-relayed oracle_crawler/r2 heartbeat channels.
# Defaults to the same slug scripts/ops_status_page.py uses.
# OPS_ALERT_GITHUB_REPO=TripAndCode/transit-app

# Where persisted alert/dedup state is kept between polls (default:
# /root/.ops-alert-state.json).
# OPS_ALERT_STATE_PATH=/root/.ops-alert-state.json

# Minimum time between repeat "reminder" alerts for a component stuck in the
# same (or a lesser) bad state (default: 1800 = 30 minutes). A fresh
# transition or an escalation to a worse state always alerts immediately,
# regardless of this interval.
# OPS_ALERT_DEDUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=1800

# How long ops-alerts.timer can go without actually running before the next
# poll reports the gap itself as a monitor-silence alert (default: 3600 = 1
# hour). Should comfortably exceed the timer's own OnCalendar= cadence.
# OPS_ALERT_MONITOR_MAX_SILENCE_SECONDS=3600

# Optional real notification endpoint. Same healthchecks.io-style convention
# oracle_cloud/v3/bin/alert-lib.sh already uses for the Oracle collector side:
# a bare ping to this URL means "quiet poll", a ping to <url>/fail (carrying
# the rendered alert as the request body) means "something is wrong". Get a
# check URL from a healthchecks.io-compatible provider (or point it at any
# endpoint that accepts a plain POST) and set it here -- see
# docs/ops-monitoring-deploy.md for the full setup. Leaving it unset is a
# fully supported configuration: delivery is skipped and the systemd exit-code
# signal (see this file's own service unit) is unchanged either way. The URL
# itself is a bearer secret (anyone holding it can post a false all-clear) --
# never commit a real value here.
# OPS_ALERT_PING_URL=
